 ## Overview
-Neural Volumes with Multi-Resolution Hash Encoding is a method for interactive volume rendering that compresses volumetric data into a neural representation using multi-resolution hash grids, enabling real-time ray tracing on modern GPUs [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from other neural field methods by supporting three distinct decoding paths—dense 3D texture, sample streaming, and in-shader inference—and by integrating macro-cell acceleration for adaptive empty-space skipping, achieving 10-60fps rendering with high fidelity (PSNR > 30dB) and compact storage (10-1000x smaller than raw data) [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [NeuS2 Network Architecture](10215-neus2-network-architecture.md) which focuses on surface reconstruction, this method targets volumetric density and color, and unlike [HashGrid Encoding](3866-hashgrid-encoding.md) which is a general feature extractor, this method provides a complete rendering pipeline optimized for volume data.
+Neural Volumes with Multi-Resolution Hash Encoding is a method for interactive volume rendering that compresses volumetric data into a neural representation using multi-resolution hash grids, enabling real-time ray tracing on modern GPUs [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from other neural field methods by supporting three distinct decoding paths—dense 3D texture, sample streaming, and in-shader inference—and by integrating macro-cell acceleration for adaptive empty-space skipping, achieving 10-60fps rendering with high fidelity (PSNR > 30dB) and compact storage (10-1000x smaller than raw data) [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [NeuS2 Network Architecture](10215-neus2-network-architecture.md) which focuses on surface reconstruction, this method targets volumetric density and color, and unlike [HashGrid Encoding](3866-hashgrid-encoding.md) which is a general feature extractor, this method provides a complete rendering pipeline optimized for volume data [S1, unofficial].
 
